    [#emph[M.K. Ranjitsinh v. UoI] 2024 INSC 280], [Right against
    adverse effects of climate change (Arts 14, 21)],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[How to use these in an exam]: Each case illustrates a different
aspect of energy law's #strong[interdisciplinary and multilevel
character]: - #emph[PTC India]: the relationship between legislative and
adjudicatory functions of regulators. - #emph[RNRL]: the tension between
private contract and public trust over natural resources. - #emph[G.
Sundarajan]: the precautionary principle as a tool for balancing energy
security against safety. - #emph[Hindustan Zinc]: the constitutional
validity of RPO as a regulatory instrument. - #emph[Energy Watchdog]:
the limits of contractual risk allocation in a changing regulatory
environment. - #emph[M.K. Ranjitsinh]: the emergence of climate rights
as a constitutional principle.

== PART B --- The Energy Trilemma
<part-b-the-energy-trilemma>
=== 2.1 Origins of the Trilemma
<origins-of-the-trilemma>
A #strong[trilemma] is three desirable objectives that cannot all be
fully achieved at once; pursuing two compromises the third (cf.~the
Mundell-Fleming "impossible trinity" in economics).

Developed by the #strong[World Energy Council] (founded 1923; a
UN-accredited body spanning about 100 countries). The three objectives
every energy system must pursue: 1. #strong[Energy security] 2.
#strong[Energy equity] (access and affordability) 3.
#strong[Environmental sustainability]

The #strong[World Energy Trilemma Index] has been published annually
since 2010; the 2024 edition ranked 126 countries.

#strong[The 2024 edition's central framing]: "Evolving with Resilience
and Justice." The three dimensions must be read against overlapping
crises: COVID-19 (2020), the Ukraine price shock (2022), climate-driven
grid failures, and for India the West Asia crisis of 2026. The WEC
explicitly recognises that the dimensions are #strong[evolving]: -
#strong[Security] now includes the reliability of weather-dependent
renewables and the availability of critical minerals. - #strong[Equity]
now includes justice in the transition itself, not only access. -
#strong[Sustainability] now extends beyond carbon to water, land,
biodiversity, and waste.

This is consistent with the Heffron-Talus "fifth stage" insight: the
trilemma is not a static framework but one that expands as new concerns
(waste, critical minerals, cyber-resilience) emerge.

=== 2.2 Pillar One: Energy Security
<pillar-one-energy-security>
#strong[IEA definition]: "The uninterrupted availability of energy
sources at an affordable price."

#strong[APERC "four A's"] (2007): Availability (do resources exist?),
Accessibility (can they be reached?), Affordability (at a bearable
price?), Acceptability (socially and environmentally tolerable?).

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (50%, 50%),
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([Traditional], [Contemporary],),
    table.hline(),
    [Reliability: keeping the lights on; adequate generation and
    grid], [Reliability of renewables: storage, demand response,
    interconnection],
    [Physical availability: domestic resources or secure
    imports], [Critical minerals (lithium, cobalt, nickel, rare earths,
    graphite) concentrated in few countries],
    [Diversification of fuels, suppliers, routes; strategic
    reserves], [Supply-chain security for modules, batteries,
    electrolysers],
    [Protection against physical attack and disaster], [Cyber-resilience
    of digital grids; climate resilience],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Illustrations]:

- #strong[2012 Indian blackout] (30--31 July): Northern, Eastern, and
  North-Eastern grids collapsed, affecting an estimated 620 million
  people in over twenty States --- the largest blackout in history. The
  enquiry found States overdrawing in breach of the Indian Electricity
  Grid Code, inter-regional lines under maintenance, and protection
  systems that failed to isolate the fault. The response: stronger
  powers for load despatch centres and tighter frequency bands.
  #strong[Reliability is a legal as much as an engineering problem.]

- #strong[Texas, February 2021]: Winter Storm Uri froze un-weatherised
  gas wells, pipelines, and power plants. Over four million Texans lost
  power for days; more than two hundred died. ERCOT's grid is
  deliberately isolated from the rest of the United States to avoid
  federal regulation, so Texas could not import power. The legislature
  then mandated weatherisation. #strong[The security cost of a
  regulatory choice made for other reasons.]

- #strong[Europe 2022]: Before 2022 Russia supplied roughly 40% of the
  EU's gas. When Nord Stream flows were cut, wholesale gas prices rose
  more than tenfold at the peak. Germany, Austria, and the Netherlands
  restarted or extended coal plants; the EU adopted REPowerEU (May
  2022); Germany built floating LNG terminals in under a year.
  #strong[Security was preserved at a cost to sustainability (more coal)
  and equity (record bills, mitigated by price caps and windfall taxes).
  The textbook trilemma trade-off.]

#strong[India's energy security profile] (from PPT):

