=== 2.3 Pillar Two: Energy Equity
<pillar-two-energy-equity>
Can all members of society access modern energy services, and can they
afford to use them?

#strong[Three elements]: accessibility (a physical connection),
affordability (adequate use without sacrificing other necessities),
universality (the last household: poor, rural, tribal, remote).

#strong[SDG 7]: "Ensure access to affordable, reliable, sustainable and
modern energy for all" by 2030.

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (50%, 50%),
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([Traditional], [Contemporary],),
    table.hline(),
    [Universal physical access to electricity and clean
    cooking], [Energy justice: fairness in decision-making and
    outcomes],
    [Affordability of tariffs and fuel prices], [Fair sharing of the
    benefits of the clean transition (jobs, clean air, savings)],
    [Cross-subsidies and lifeline tariffs for the vulnerable], [Just
    transition for fossil-fuel-dependent communities and workers],
    [Quality and reliability of supply for all], [Gender: the
    biomass-cooking burden on women and girls],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Global picture] (Tracking SDG 7, 2025): ≈ 655 million people
without electricity; ≈ 2.1 billion without clean cooking fuels and
technologies; ≈ 6.7 million deaths a year from ambient and household air
pollution (WHO). Household air pollution from wood, dung, and crop
residue is a leading cause of respiratory disease, borne
disproportionately by women and children.

#strong[Accessibility in India]: - DDUGJY (2014): last un-electrified
villages connected by April 2018. - Saubhagya (2017): 2.86 crore
households connected by March 2019. - PMUY (2016): over 10 crore LPG
connections to poor households; ₹300 subsidy per 14.2 kg cylinder for up
to nine refills in 2025--26. - #strong[But access is not use]: NFHS-5
indicates roughly four in ten households (over half in rural India)
still cook mainly with solid fuels ("fuel stacking"), because refills
are unaffordable or distant.

#strong[Affordability: "energy burden"]. Energy burden = share of
household income spent on energy. US literature (Fisher, Sheehan and
Colton) treats over 6% as high and over 10% as severe; India has no
official threshold but the concept applies. Energy is a necessity with
few substitutes, so the burden falls hardest on the poor; rising
temperatures are turning cooling from a luxury into a survival need.

