    connection],
    [Cross-subsidies], [ss. 61(g), 62(3); Tariff Policy 2016 (±20% of
    average cost)], [Domestic and agricultural tariffs below cost],
    [Direct subsidy], [s. 65: State must pay the licensee in
    advance], [Free or subsidised power for farmers and the poor],
    [Consumer rights], [Electricity (Rights of Consumers) Rules
    2020], [Time-bound connections; reliability; prosumers],
    [Access schemes], [Saubhagya; PMUY; PM Surya Ghar], [Physical
    access; bill relief],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Cross-subsidisation: how it works]: - Industrial and commercial
users pay #strong[above cost]. - Domestic and agricultural users pay
#strong[below cost]. - The DISCOM is the intermediary.

Born of equity: affordable power for farmers and the poor.
#strong[Cost]: industrial tariffs of ₹7--10 per unit push large users to
captive plants and open access; the ±20% Tariff Policy band is breached;
DISCOM borrowings ≈ ₹7.26 lakh crore (March 2025, PFC).

#strong[Draft Electricity (Amendment) Bill, 2025] (released by Ministry
of Power for public comment on 9 October 2025): Proposals include
elimination of cross-subsidies for manufacturing, railways, and metro
railways within five years; tariffs that "reflect the cost of supply"\;
hidden cross-subsidies replaced by transparent, budgeted subsidies under
s. 65; continued protection for farmers and low-income households.
#strong[Status]: still a draft at the time of writing; not enacted.

#strong[From equity to energy justice]: Contemporary scholarship deepens
"equity" into "energy justice" with three tenets (developed in Part C).

