#strong[Sustainability versus sustainability]: Decarbonisation can
collide with other environmental values. The Great Indian Bustard
litigation pitted solar and wind expansion against a critically
endangered bird. Himalayan hydro (Subansiri, Teesta) pits low-carbon
power against seismic risk, sediment, and displacement. Lithium
extraction in Chile's Salar de Atacama pits global electrification
against the water rights of indigenous Atacameño communities.
#strong[Sustainability is a bundle of values that must itself be
balanced.]

=== 2.5 The World Energy Trilemma Index
<the-world-energy-trilemma-index>
Published by the World Energy Council with Oliver Wyman since 2010; 2024
was the fifteenth edition, covering 126 countries.

Each dimension scored from a basket of indicators (IEA, World Bank, IMF
data); a country-context adjustment reflects governance and
macroeconomic stability. Outputs: overall rank and score; a letter grade
(A--D) per dimension; a "balance triangle" whose shape shows the
neglected dimension; time series from 2000.

#strong[Top ten, 2024]: Denmark (1), Sweden (2), Finland (3),
Switzerland (4), Canada (5), Austria (6), France (7), Germany (8),
Estonia (9), United Kingdom (10).

#strong[Leaders share]: high low-carbon shares (wind in Denmark; hydro
and nuclear in Sweden and Finland; hydro in Switzerland and Canada),
strong interconnection, and high incomes that make energy affordable.

#strong[India]: marked improvement on equity (access schemes), weaker on
security (import dependence) and sustainability (coal), placing it in
the lower-middle of the ranking.

#strong[National Energy Trilemma Index (NETI) 2024]: Weights: security
25%, equity 25%, sustainability 25%, state context 25%. Top states:
Kerala (65.08), Gujarat (62.71), Karnataka (61.33), Tamil Nadu (58.70),
Himachal Pradesh (57.95), Andhra Pradesh (57.60), Haryana (57.12),
Uttarakhand (57.10), Maharashtra (57.07), Goa (57.06).

#strong[What NETI shows]: Dimension leaders: Gujarat (security and
equity), Kerala (environmental sustainability), Karnataka (state
context). The gap between top and bottom is wide: national averages
conceal as much as they reveal. This is why the Electricity Act's
federal design matters for the trilemma: distribution, subsidies, and
RPO enforcement are State functions.

#strong[Other measures]: Energy Transition Index (WEF); Tracking SDG 7
(IEA, IRENA, UNSD, World Bank, WHO); World Energy Outlook (IEA); Climate
Change Performance Index (Germanwatch); State Energy Efficiency Index
(BEE and AEEE).

#strong[Limits of measurement]: Weights are subjective; data lag by two
to three years. Indices reward wealth: rich countries find affordability
easy. A country is treated as one unit, hiding regional inequality
(hence NETI). Resilience is invisible until a crisis reveals it.
#strong[Use an index as a diagnostic map of where a legal system's
attention lies, not as a verdict.]

=== 2.6 How Laws Balance the Trilemma
<how-laws-balance-the-trilemma>
#strong[The long title of the Electricity Act 2003] pursues all three
goals at once: "promoting competition … protecting interest of consumers
and supply of electricity to all areas, rationalisation of electricity
tariff, ensuring transparent policies regarding subsidies, promotion of
efficient and environmentally benign policies."

- Competition and rational tariffs → security and efficiency.
- Supply to all areas and transparent subsidies → equity.
- Environmentally benign policies → sustainability.

