#strong[Germany's Energiewende]: After Fukushima (2011) Germany
legislated a nuclear exit, closing its last three reactors in April 2023
while expanding wind and solar. When Russian gas was cut in 2022, the
loss of firm low-carbon capacity meant lignite generation rose and
prices hit records. #strong[A legally binding sustainability commitment
produced unintended security and sustainability consequences when
circumstances changed.]

#strong[South Africa's load shedding]: Eskom, burdened by debt, ageing
coal plants, and governance failures, imposed rolling blackouts that
peaked in 2023 at more than 300 days of outages. The wealthy installed
solar, batteries, and generators; townships bore the cuts: "energy
apartheid." #strong[A security failure became an equity failure, while
extending coal-plant lives undermined sustainability.]

#strong[Cross-subsidy: the trilemma in miniature]: - #strong[Energy
equity (+)]: Affordable power for farmers and low-income households; a
social necessity, not merely an inefficiency. - #strong[Energy security
(−)]: DISCOM borrowings ≈ ₹7.26 lakh crore (March 2025); no money for
the network or adequate peak power; large consumers leave the grid. -
#strong[Environmental sustainability (−)]: Free power encourages waste
and groundwater over-pumping; weak DISCOMs slow to integrate renewables.

#strong[The way forward: a phased, targeted approach]: - Phase out
gradually: abrupt withdrawal causes hardship and backlash (the Draft
Bill's five-year horizon). - Target precisely: subsidise the poor and
marginal farmers, not every domestic and agricultural consumer. - Make
subsidies transparent: s. 65 advance payment; direct benefit transfer
(LPG under PAHAL; Punjab's agricultural power pilot). - Reinvest
recovered revenue in grid modernisation, storage, and renewable
integration. - Build consensus with States, farmers, industry, and
DISCOM staff: electricity is a Concurrent subject and distribution a
State function.

== PART C --- Energy Justice: The Human Dimension
<part-c-energy-justice-the-human-dimension>
=== 3.1 What Energy Justice Adds
<what-energy-justice-adds>
Developed by Sovacool, Heffron, McCauley, Jenkins, and others; deepens
the equity pillar of the trilemma. Energy justice asks: #strong[who
benefits and who bears the costs; who decides and who is excluded; whose
identities are recognised, at every stage from mining to waste.]

The energy justice framework maps onto legal doctrine: Articles 14, 38,
39 (distribution); natural justice, EIA public hearings, Forest Rights
Act consent (procedure); Fifth and Sixth Schedules, PESA 1996, UNDRIP
(recognition).

#strong[Heffron and Talus] emphasise that energy justice is not merely a
normative add-on but a #strong[theoretical framework] that can guide
legal analysis. It allows scholars and practitioners to evaluate whether
a given energy law or policy is #strong[just] --- not just whether it is
efficient or legally valid.

=== 3.2 The Three Tenets
<the-three-tenets>
#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (33.33%, 33.33%, 33.33%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Tenet], [Central question], [Indian legal anchors],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Distributive]], [Are benefits and burdens fairly
    shared?], [Arts 14, 38, 39(b); District Mineral Foundation (s. 9B
    MMDR); rehabilitation],
    [#strong[Procedural]], [Are decisions transparent, participatory,
    with remedies?], [EIA public hearing; gram sabha consent (FRA 2006,
    PESA); s. 64 Electricity Act; NGT],
    [#strong[Recognition]], [Are the rights and cultures of all groups
    acknowledged?], [Fifth and Sixth Schedules; #emph[Samatha] (1997);
    #emph[Niyamgiri] (2013)],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Distributive justice: lithium in the Atacama]. Chile holds the
world's largest lithium reserves. SQM and Albemarle pump brine from
beneath the Salar de Atacama and evaporate it. The world gets cheaper EV
batteries; companies and the State get revenue. Indigenous Atacameño
communities who depend on scarce water bear falling water tables and
degraded wetlands. Courts and the regulator have imposed sanctions and
consultation; the 2023 National Lithium Strategy proposes State
participation and benefit sharing.

#strong[Procedural justice: Kudankulam]. Fishing communities near the
Kudankulam plant in Tamil Nadu protested after Fukushima, arguing they
had not been consulted on safety, evacuation, and spent fuel. In
#emph[G. Sundarajan v. Union of India] (2013) 6 SCC 620, the Supreme
Court upheld commissioning, balancing energy needs against safety
through the precautionary principle, and directed safety measures,
spent-fuel storage, and local involvement. #strong[The case shows the
limits of judicial review as a forum for participation.]

#strong[Recognition justice]. In the Atacama the deeper injustice is
that the Atacameño view of water and land as sacred was never given
weight in decisions framed in tonnes and emissions. India's coal
districts (Jharkhand, Odisha, Chhattisgarh, West Bengal) hold large
populations of informal workers (contract miners, loaders, transporters)
absent from payrolls; just-transition plans counting only formal
employees leave them behind. Jharkhand set up India's first Sustainable
Just Transition Task Force in 2022.

=== 3.3 The Cycle of Energy Injustice
<the-cycle-of-energy-injustice>
```
ENERGY POVERTY (no access to clean energy)
        ↓ forced onto costly fuels
ENERGY BURDEN (high share of income)
        ↓ under-use, fuel stacking
ENERGY INSECURITY (unreliable supply)
        ↓ democracy breaks the cycle
ENERGY DEMOCRACY (community control)
```

#strong[The four components defined]:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (33.33%, 33.33%, 33.33%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Component], [Definition], [Indian illustration],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Energy poverty]], [No access to modern, clean, safe energy
    for basic needs], [About four in ten households still cook mainly
    with biomass],
    [#strong[Energy burden]], [High share of income spent on
    energy], [Nagpur, April 2026: ₹1,960 bill ≈ a tenth of income],
    [#strong[Energy insecurity]], [Cannot rely on nominal supply:
    outages, voltage, disconnection], [Non-solar-hour shortfalls in the
    May 2026 heatwave (270.8 GW peak)],
    [#strong[Energy democracy]], [Community control over production,
    distribution, use], [Solar cooperatives; panchayat storage; P2P
    trading pilots],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Energy democracy: SPICE, Dhundi (Gujarat)]. In 2016 six farmers
in Dhundi village, Kheda district, formed the world's first Solar Pump
Irrigators' Cooperative Enterprise with the IWMI-Tata programme. Solar
pumps replaced diesel; surplus power is sold to the State DISCOM (MGVCL)
under a 25-year PPA at about ₹4.63 per unit. #strong[Solar becomes a
"remunerative crop"\; over-pumping of groundwater is discouraged because
pumping now has an opportunity cost; farmers, not a distant utility,
control the asset.]

#strong[Three more examples]: - #strong[Modhera] (Mehsana, Gujarat)
became India's first round-the-clock solar village in 2022: about 1,300
rooftop systems, a 6 MW plant, and a 15 MWh battery. MNRE has earmarked
₹800 crore for one Model Solar Village per district (₹1 crore each). -
#strong[Perinjanam panchayat] (Thrissur, Kerala) has put rooftop solar
on about a thousand homes since 2017 via a consumers' committee and
cooperative-bank loans, and is building India's first panchayat-level
battery storage with ₹2 crore of State support. - #strong[UPERC
peer-to-peer trading pilot] (23 February 2026): India's first interstate
P2P renewable trading pilot (PVVNL area to Delhi DISCOM consumers) on a
blockchain platform under the India Energy Stack, wheeling charge ₹1.01
per unit; extended to March 2027.

#strong[Policies for the four components]:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (33.33%, 33.33%, 33.33%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Component], [Policy], [Key features],),
    table.hline(),
    [Energy burden], [PM Surya Ghar (2024); AP weavers; Maharashtra;
    Bihar], [Rooftop solar and free units for low-income and
    occupational groups],
    [Energy insecurity], [Emergency measures, March 2026], [LPG
