#strong[How the components interact: the PMUY cycle]: - #strong[Energy
poverty]: millions of rural households cooked with biomass. -
#strong[PMUY] provided free connections, removing the access barrier. -
#strong[Energy burden]: even a subsidised refill is a large outlay for a
household earning a few thousand rupees a month. - #strong[Energy
insecurity]: households "stack" fuels, reverting to biomass when cash is
short, so the health benefit is only partly realised. - #strong[Energy
democracy]: community-scale solutions (biogas cooperatives, collective
LPG procurement, electric cooking on rooftop solar) address what
connection alone cannot.

=== 3.4 Two Logics (Mohan and Topp, 2018)
<two-logics-mohan-and-topp-2018>
The PPT presents this as a binary, but the source article makes clear
that these are #strong[contested narratives] in Indian energy policy,
not mutually exclusive positions.

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (33.33%, 33.33%, 33.33%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Feature], ["Energy for growth"], ["Energy for all"],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Conception of energy]], [A productive input for industry
    and GDP], [A basic human need and condition of dignity],
    [#strong[Central question]], [How do we keep energy from
    constraining growth?], [How do we ensure no one is left behind?],
    [#strong[Governance style]], [Centralised, technocratic,
    top-down], [Participatory, decentralised, rights-based],
    [#strong[Investment focus]], [Ultra-mega plants, national grid,
    pipelines], [Last-mile connections, decentralised renewables],
    [#strong[View of cross-subsidy]], [A distortion that drives large
    consumers off-grid], [A necessary social intervention],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Source context]: Mohan and Topp (2018) identify two dominant
narratives in Indian energy policy debates: "energy for development"
that privileges energy as critical to economic growth and long-term
strategic security, and "energy for all" that prioritises the role of
energy for basic development and ending poverty. While these narratives
find common ground on certain issues such as the role of coal, they
clash in the socio-technical imaginaries they represent about India's
energy future.

#strong[Why this matters for the exam]: The trilemma (security, equity,
sustainability) is often presented as a technical balancing exercise.
Mohan and Topp show that the "balance" is actually a #strong[political
contest] between competing narratives about what energy is for. Energy
law is the terrain on which this contest is fought --- through RPO
regulations, tariff orders, environmental clearances, and
just-transition plans.

